<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" standalone="yes"?><rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><channel><title>GoNumericTypes - Tag - loommii</title><link>https://loommii.github.io/en/tags/gonumerictypes/</link><description>我的个人技术博客</description><generator>Hugo 0.165.0 &amp; FixIt v0.4.5</generator><language>en</language><lastBuildDate>Fri, 16 Jan 2026 01:28:43 +0800</lastBuildDate><atom:link href="https://loommii.github.io/en/tags/gonumerictypes/index.xml" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><item><title>Go Daily Question 203</title><link>https://loommii.github.io/en/go_practise/go_daily_question_203/</link><pubDate>Tue, 04 Mar 2025 21:11:41 +0800</pubDate><guid>https://loommii.github.io/en/go_practise/go_daily_question_203/</guid><category domain="https://loommii.github.io/en/categories/godailyquestion/">GoDailyQuestion</category><category domain="https://loommii.github.io/en/categories/golanguagebasics/">GoLanguageBasics</category><description>&lt;p&gt;Today, here are two similar problems; note that more than half of people might get them wrong!&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;Problem 1:&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;package main&#10;&#10;func main() {&#10; var a int8 = -1&#10; var b int8 = -128 / a&#10;&#10; println(b)&#10;}&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;p&gt;Problem 2:&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;package main&#10;&#10;func main() {&#10; const a int8 = -1&#10; var b int8 = -128 / a&#10;&#10; println(b)&#10;}&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;p&gt;Will the above two problems compile? If they compile, what will be the output?&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Go Daily Question 171</title><link>https://loommii.github.io/en/go_practise/go_daily_question_171/</link><pubDate>Sat, 25 Jan 2025 23:19:23 +0800</pubDate><guid>https://loommii.github.io/en/go_practise/go_daily_question_171/</guid><category domain="https://loommii.github.io/en/categories/godailyquestion/">GoDailyQuestion</category><category domain="https://loommii.github.io/en/categories/golanguagebasics/">GoLanguageBasics</category><description>&lt;p&gt;What is the output of the following code?&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;package main&#10;&#10;import (&#10; &amp;#34;fmt&amp;#34;&#10;)&#10;&#10;func main() {&#10; var ans float64 = 15 &amp;#43; 25 &amp;#43; 5.2&#10; fmt.Println(ans)&#10;}&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;ul&gt;&#10;&lt;li&gt;A: Cannot compile;&lt;/li&gt;&#10;&lt;li&gt;B: 45;&lt;/li&gt;&#10;&lt;li&gt;C: 45.2;&lt;/li&gt;&#10;&lt;li&gt;D: 45.0&lt;/li&gt;&#10;&lt;/ul&gt;&#10;&#10;&lt;details&gt;&#10; &lt;summary&gt;🔑 Answer Analysis:&lt;/summary&gt;&#10; &lt;p&gt;C&#10;&lt;a href="https://go.dev/play/p/VCR1yAY32nG" target="_blank" rel="external nofollow noopener noreferrer"&gt;Run online&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;h2 class="heading-element" id="analysis"&gt;&lt;span&gt;Analysis&lt;/span&gt;&#10; &lt;a href="#analysis" class="heading-mark"&gt;&#10; &lt;svg class="octicon octicon-link" viewBox="0 0 16 16" version="1.1" width="16" height="16" aria-hidden="true"&gt;&lt;path d="m7.775 3.275 1.25-1.25a3.5 3.5 0 1 1 4.95 4.95l-2.5 2.5a3.5 3.5 0 0 1-4.95 0 .751.751 0 0 1 .018-1.042.751.751 0 0 1 1.042-.018 1.998 1.998 0 0 0 2.83 0l2.5-2.5a2.002 2.002 0 0 0-2.83-2.83l-1.25 1.25a.751.751 0 0 1-1.042-.018.751.751 0 0 1-.018-1.042Zm-4.69 9.64a1.998 1.998 0 0 0 2.83 0l1.25-1.25a.751.751 0 0 1 1.042.018.751.751 0 0 1 .018 1.042l-1.25 1.25a3.5 3.5 0 1 1-4.95-4.95l2.5-2.5a3.5 3.5 0 0 1 4.95 0 .751.751 0 0 1-.018 1.042.751.751 0 0 1-1.042.018 1.998 1.998 0 0 0-2.83 0l-2.5 2.5a1.998 1.998 0 0 0 0 2.83Z"&gt;&lt;/path&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;&#10; &lt;/a&gt;&#10;&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;h3 class="heading-element" id="1-constant-expressions"&gt;&lt;span&gt;1. Constant Expressions&lt;/span&gt;&#10; &lt;a href="#1-constant-expressions" class="heading-mark"&gt;&#10; &lt;svg class="octicon octicon-link" viewBox="0 0 16 16" version="1.1" width="16" height="16" aria-hidden="true"&gt;&lt;path d="m7.775 3.275 1.25-1.25a3.5 3.5 0 1 1 4.95 4.95l-2.5 2.5a3.5 3.5 0 0 1-4.95 0 .751.751 0 0 1 .018-1.042.751.751 0 0 1 1.042-.018 1.998 1.998 0 0 0 2.83 0l2.5-2.5a2.002 2.002 0 0 0-2.83-2.83l-1.25 1.25a.751.751 0 0 1-1.042-.018.751.751 0 0 1-.018-1.042Zm-4.69 9.64a1.998 1.998 0 0 0 2.83 0l1.25-1.25a.751.751 0 0 1 1.042.018.751.751 0 0 1 .018 1.042l-1.25 1.25a3.5 3.5 0 1 1-4.95-4.95l2.5-2.5a3.5 3.5 0 0 1 4.95 0 .751.751 0 0 1-.018 1.042.751.751 0 0 1-1.042.018 1.998 1.998 0 0 0-2.83 0l-2.5 2.5a1.998 1.998 0 0 0 0 2.83Z"&gt;&lt;/path&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;&#10; &lt;/a&gt;&#10;&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;ul&gt;&#10;&lt;li&gt;A &lt;strong&gt;constant expression&lt;/strong&gt; is an expression that contains only constant operands and is evaluated at compile time.&lt;/li&gt;&#10;&lt;li&gt;Constants can be:&#10;&lt;ul&gt;&#10;&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Untyped constants&lt;/strong&gt;: e.g., &lt;code&gt;1&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;2&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;5.2&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;&#10;&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Typed constants&lt;/strong&gt;: e.g., &lt;code&gt;const b int8 = 3&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;&#10;&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Literal constants&lt;/strong&gt;: e.g., &lt;code&gt;1&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;2&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;5.2&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;&#10;&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Named constants&lt;/strong&gt;: e.g., &lt;code&gt;const a = 1 + 2&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;&#10;&lt;/ul&gt;&#10;&lt;/li&gt;&#10;&lt;/ul&gt;&#10;&lt;h3 class="heading-element" id="2-default-types-of-untyped-constants"&gt;&lt;span&gt;2. Default Types of Untyped Constants&lt;/span&gt;&#10; &lt;a href="#2-default-types-of-untyped-constants" class="heading-mark"&gt;&#10; &lt;svg class="octicon octicon-link" viewBox="0 0 16 16" version="1.1" width="16" height="16" aria-hidden="true"&gt;&lt;path d="m7.775 3.275 1.25-1.25a3.5 3.5 0 1 1 4.95 4.95l-2.5 2.5a3.5 3.5 0 0 1-4.95 0 .751.751 0 0 1 .018-1.042.751.751 0 0 1 1.042-.018 1.998 1.998 0 0 0 2.83 0l2.5-2.5a2.002 2.002 0 0 0-2.83-2.83l-1.25 1.25a.751.751 0 0 1-1.042-.018.751.751 0 0 1-.018-1.042Zm-4.69 9.64a1.998 1.998 0 0 0 2.83 0l1.25-1.25a.751.751 0 0 1 1.042.018.751.751 0 0 1 .018 1.042l-1.25 1.25a3.5 3.5 0 1 1-4.95-4.95l2.5-2.5a3.5 3.5 0 0 1 4.95 0 .751.751 0 0 1-.018 1.042.751.751 0 0 1-1.042.018 1.998 1.998 0 0 0-2.83 0l-2.5 2.5a1.998 1.998 0 0 0 0 2.83Z"&gt;&lt;/path&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;&#10; &lt;/a&gt;&#10;&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;ul&gt;&#10;&lt;li&gt;Although untyped constants do not have an explicit type, they have a default type:&#10;&lt;ul&gt;&#10;&lt;li&gt;Integer literals (e.g., &lt;code&gt;15&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;25&lt;/code&gt;) have a default type of &lt;code&gt;int&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;&#10;&lt;li&gt;Floating-point literals (e.g., &lt;code&gt;5.2&lt;/code&gt;) have a default type of &lt;code&gt;float64&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;&#10;&lt;li&gt;String literals have a default type of &lt;code&gt;string&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;&#10;&lt;li&gt;Boolean literals have a default type of &lt;code&gt;bool&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;&#10;&lt;li&gt;Complex literals have a default type of &lt;code&gt;complex128&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;&#10;&lt;/ul&gt;&#10;&lt;/li&gt;&#10;&lt;/ul&gt;&#10;&lt;h3 class="heading-element" id="3-type-inference"&gt;&lt;span&gt;3. Type Inference&lt;/span&gt;&#10; &lt;a href="#3-type-inference" class="heading-mark"&gt;&#10; &lt;svg class="octicon octicon-link" viewBox="0 0 16 16" version="1.1" width="16" height="16" aria-hidden="true"&gt;&lt;path d="m7.775 3.275 1.25-1.25a3.5 3.5 0 1 1 4.95 4.95l-2.5 2.5a3.5 3.5 0 0 1-4.95 0 .751.751 0 0 1 .018-1.042.751.751 0 0 1 1.042-.018 1.998 1.998 0 0 0 2.83 0l2.5-2.5a2.002 2.002 0 0 0-2.83-2.83l-1.25 1.25a.751.751 0 0 1-1.042-.018.751.751 0 0 1-.018-1.042Zm-4.69 9.64a1.998 1.998 0 0 0 2.83 0l1.25-1.25a.751.751 0 0 1 1.042.018.751.751 0 0 1 .018 1.042l-1.25 1.25a3.5 3.5 0 1 1-4.95-4.95l2.5-2.5a3.5 3.5 0 0 1 4.95 0 .751.751 0 0 1-.018 1.042.751.751 0 0 1-1.042.018 1.998 1.998 0 0 0-2.83 0l-2.5 2.5a1.998 1.998 0 0 0 0 2.83Z"&gt;&lt;/path&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;&#10; &lt;/a&gt;&#10;&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;ul&gt;&#10;&lt;li&gt;Go language supports type inference. If the context does not specify an explicit type, the compiler will treat untyped constants as their default type.&lt;/li&gt;&#10;&lt;li&gt;In a constant expression, if the operands include untyped constants of different types, the result&amp;rsquo;s type will be inferred according to the following precedence:&#10;&lt;ul&gt;&#10;&lt;li&gt;Integer &amp;lt; rune &amp;lt; float &amp;lt; complex.&lt;/li&gt;&#10;&lt;/ul&gt;&#10;&lt;/li&gt;&#10;&lt;/ul&gt;&#10;&lt;h3 class="heading-element" id="4-constant-expression-in-the-problem"&gt;&lt;span&gt;4. Constant Expression in the Problem&lt;/span&gt;&#10; &lt;a href="#4-constant-expression-in-the-problem" class="heading-mark"&gt;&#10; &lt;svg class="octicon octicon-link" viewBox="0 0 16 16" version="1.1" width="16" height="16" aria-hidden="true"&gt;&lt;path d="m7.775 3.275 1.25-1.25a3.5 3.5 0 1 1 4.95 4.95l-2.5 2.5a3.5 3.5 0 0 1-4.95 0 .751.751 0 0 1 .018-1.042.751.751 0 0 1 1.042-.018 1.998 1.998 0 0 0 2.83 0l2.5-2.5a2.002 2.002 0 0 0-2.83-2.83l-1.25 1.25a.751.751 0 0 1-1.042-.018.751.751 0 0 1-.018-1.042Zm-4.69 9.64a1.998 1.998 0 0 0 2.83 0l1.25-1.25a.751.751 0 0 1 1.042.018.751.751 0 0 1 .018 1.042l-1.25 1.25a3.5 3.5 0 1 1-4.95-4.95l2.5-2.5a3.5 3.5 0 0 1 4.95 0 .751.751 0 0 1-.018 1.042.751.751 0 0 1-1.042.018 1.998 1.998 0 0 0-2.83 0l-2.5 2.5a1.998 1.998 0 0 0 0 2.83Z"&gt;&lt;/path&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;&#10; &lt;/a&gt;&#10;&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;var ans float64 = 15 &amp;#43; 25 &amp;#43; 5.2&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;ul&gt;&#10;&lt;li&gt;&lt;code&gt;15&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;25&lt;/code&gt; are untyped integer constants, with a default type of &lt;code&gt;int&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;&#10;&lt;li&gt;&lt;code&gt;5.2&lt;/code&gt; is an untyped floating-point constant, with a default type of &lt;code&gt;float64&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;&#10;&lt;li&gt;In the constant expression, since &lt;code&gt;5.2&lt;/code&gt; is a floating-point constant, the entire expression&amp;rsquo;s result will be inferred as a floating-point type (&lt;code&gt;float64&lt;/code&gt;).&lt;/li&gt;&#10;&lt;/ul&gt;&#10;&lt;h3 class="heading-element" id="operation-process"&gt;&lt;span&gt;Operation Process&lt;/span&gt;&#10; &lt;a href="#operation-process" class="heading-mark"&gt;&#10; &lt;svg class="octicon octicon-link" viewBox="0 0 16 16" version="1.1" width="16" height="16" aria-hidden="true"&gt;&lt;path d="m7.775 3.275 1.25-1.25a3.5 3.5 0 1 1 4.95 4.95l-2.5 2.5a3.5 3.5 0 0 1-4.95 0 .751.751 0 0 1 .018-1.042.751.751 0 0 1 1.042-.018 1.998 1.998 0 0 0 2.83 0l2.5-2.5a2.002 2.002 0 0 0-2.83-2.83l-1.25 1.25a.751.751 0 0 1-1.042-.018.751.751 0 0 1-.018-1.042Zm-4.69 9.64a1.998 1.998 0 0 0 2.83 0l1.25-1.25a.751.751 0 0 1 1.042.018.751.751 0 0 1 .018 1.042l-1.25 1.25a3.5 3.5 0 1 1-4.95-4.95l2.5-2.5a3.5 3.5 0 0 1 4.95 0 .751.751 0 0 1-.018 1.042.751.751 0 0 1-1.042.018 1.998 1.998 0 0 0-2.83 0l-2.5 2.5a1.998 1.998 0 0 0 0 2.83Z"&gt;&lt;/path&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;&#10; &lt;/a&gt;&#10;&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;ul&gt;&#10;&lt;li&gt;The result of &lt;code&gt;15 + 25&lt;/code&gt; is &lt;code&gt;40&lt;/code&gt;, which is still an untyped integer constant.&lt;/li&gt;&#10;&lt;li&gt;The result of &lt;code&gt;40 + 5.2&lt;/code&gt; is &lt;code&gt;45.2&lt;/code&gt;. Since &lt;code&gt;5.2&lt;/code&gt; is a floating-point constant, the entire expression&amp;rsquo;s result is inferred as &lt;code&gt;float64&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;&#10;&lt;/ul&gt;&#10;&lt;h3 class="heading-element" id="assignment-and-output"&gt;&lt;span&gt;Assignment and Output&lt;/span&gt;&#10; &lt;a href="#assignment-and-output" class="heading-mark"&gt;&#10; &lt;svg class="octicon octicon-link" viewBox="0 0 16 16" version="1.1" width="16" height="16" aria-hidden="true"&gt;&lt;path d="m7.775 3.275 1.25-1.25a3.5 3.5 0 1 1 4.95 4.95l-2.5 2.5a3.5 3.5 0 0 1-4.95 0 .751.751 0 0 1 .018-1.042.751.751 0 0 1 1.042-.018 1.998 1.998 0 0 0 2.83 0l2.5-2.5a2.002 2.002 0 0 0-2.83-2.83l-1.25 1.25a.751.751 0 0 1-1.042-.018.751.751 0 0 1-.018-1.042Zm-4.69 9.64a1.998 1.998 0 0 0 2.83 0l1.25-1.25a.751.751 0 0 1 1.042.018.751.751 0 0 1 .018 1.042l-1.25 1.25a3.5 3.5 0 1 1-4.95-4.95l2.5-2.5a3.5 3.5 0 0 1 4.95 0 .751.751 0 0 1-.018 1.042.751.751 0 0 1-1.042.018 1.998 1.998 0 0 0-2.83 0l-2.5 2.5a1.998 1.998 0 0 0 0 2.83Z"&gt;&lt;/path&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;&#10; &lt;/a&gt;&#10;&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;ul&gt;&#10;&lt;li&gt;The final result &lt;code&gt;45.2&lt;/code&gt; is assigned to the variable &lt;code&gt;ans&lt;/code&gt;, whose type is &lt;code&gt;float64&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;&#10;&lt;li&gt;&lt;code&gt;fmt.Println(ans)&lt;/code&gt; outputs &lt;code&gt;45.2&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;&#10;&lt;/ul&gt;&#10;&lt;/details&gt;</description></item><item><title>Go Daily Question 143</title><link>https://loommii.github.io/en/go_practise/go_daily_question_143/</link><pubDate>Thu, 26 Dec 2024 20:28:59 +0800</pubDate><guid>https://loommii.github.io/en/go_practise/go_daily_question_143/</guid><category domain="https://loommii.github.io/en/categories/godailyquestion/">GoDailyQuestion</category><category domain="https://loommii.github.io/en/categories/golanguagebasics/">GoLanguageBasics</category><description>&lt;p&gt;What is the output of the following code?&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;package main&#10;&#10;import &amp;#34;fmt&amp;#34;&#10;&#10;func main() {&#10; i := -5&#10; j := &amp;#43;5&#10; fmt.Printf(&amp;#34;%&amp;#43;d %&amp;#43;d&amp;#34;, i, j)&#10;}&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;ul&gt;&#10;&lt;li&gt;A. -5 +5&lt;/li&gt;&#10;&lt;li&gt;B. +5 +5&lt;/li&gt;&#10;&lt;li&gt;C. -5 5&lt;/li&gt;&#10;&lt;li&gt;D. +5 -5&lt;/li&gt;&#10;&lt;/ul&gt;&#10;&#10;&lt;details&gt;&#10; &lt;summary&gt;🔑 Answer Analysis:&lt;/summary&gt;&#10; &lt;p&gt;A&#10;&lt;a href="https://go.dev/play/p/vDR1EXgEiDa" target="_blank" rel="external nofollow noopener noreferrer"&gt;Run online&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;According to the official documentation: &lt;a href="https://pkg.go.dev/fmt" target="_blank" rel="external nofollow noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://pkg.go.dev/fmt&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;%b Binary&#10;%c Character represented by the corresponding Unicode code point&#10;%d Decimal&#10;%o Octal&#10;%O Octal with 0o prefix&#10;%q Single-quoted character literal safely escaped using Go syntax&#10;%x Hexadecimal, a-f in lowercase&#10;%X Hexadecimal, A-F in uppercase&#10;%U Unicode format: U&amp;#43;1234; equivalent to &amp;#34;U&amp;#43;%04X&amp;#34;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;&amp;#43;: Always print the sign for numbers; for %q (%&amp;#43;q), guarantees to output only ASCII characters.&#10;-: Pad with spaces on the right rather than left (left-justify field).&#10;#: Alternate format: binary (%#b) adds leading 0b, octal (%#o) adds leading 0, hexadecimal (%#x or %#X) adds leading 0x or 0X; for %p (%#p), does not show leading 0x; for %q, if [strconv.CanBackquote] returns true, prints the raw (backquote-enclosed) string; for %e, %E, %f, %F, %g, and %G, always print a decimal point; for %g and %G, do not remove trailing zeros; for %U (%#U), if the character is printable, for example, written as U&amp;#43;0078 &amp;#39;x&amp;#39;.&#10;(space): For numbers, leave a space for an omitted sign (% d); when printing strings or slices in hexadecimal, add spaces between bytes (% x, % X).&#10;0: Pad with leading zeros rather than spaces; for numbers, this moves the padding after the sign.&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;p&gt;Returning to this problem, &lt;code&gt;%+d&lt;/code&gt;. &lt;code&gt;d&lt;/code&gt; for decimal and &lt;code&gt;+&lt;/code&gt;: always print the sign for numbers.&#10;Combined, it means printing a decimal number with its sign, so the output is:&#10;A. -5 +5&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Go Daily Question 134</title><link>https://loommii.github.io/en/go_practise/go_daily_question_134/</link><pubDate>Tue, 17 Dec 2024 14:00:22 +0800</pubDate><guid>https://loommii.github.io/en/go_practise/go_daily_question_134/</guid><category domain="https://loommii.github.io/en/categories/godailyquestion/">GoDailyQuestion</category><category domain="https://loommii.github.io/en/categories/golanguagebasics/">GoLanguageBasics</category><description>&lt;p&gt;What is the output of the following code?&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;func main() { &#10; a := 5&#10; b := 8.1&#10; fmt.Println(a &amp;#43; b)&#10;}&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;ul&gt;&#10;&lt;li&gt;A.13.1&lt;/li&gt;&#10;&lt;li&gt;B.13&lt;/li&gt;&#10;&lt;li&gt;C.compilation error&lt;/li&gt;&#10;&lt;/ul&gt;&#10;&#10;&lt;details&gt;&#10; &lt;summary&gt;🔑 Answer Analysis:&lt;/summary&gt;&#10; &lt;p&gt;Answer: C. Run online: &lt;a href="https://go.dev/play/p/VDTgpXoukTb" target="_blank" rel="external nofollow noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://go.dev/play/p/VDTgpXoukTb&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;In Go, there is no implicit type conversion between variables. However, the compiler can perform implicit type conversion between variables and constants.&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;In the problem, &lt;code&gt;a&lt;/code&gt; is an &lt;code&gt;int&lt;/code&gt; variable and &lt;code&gt;b&lt;/code&gt; is a &lt;code&gt;float64&lt;/code&gt; variable.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Go Daily Question 122</title><link>https://loommii.github.io/en/go_practise/go_daily_question_122/</link><pubDate>Fri, 06 Dec 2024 23:43:05 +0800</pubDate><guid>https://loommii.github.io/en/go_practise/go_daily_question_122/</guid><category domain="https://loommii.github.io/en/categories/godailyquestion/">GoDailyQuestion</category><category domain="https://loommii.github.io/en/categories/golanguagebasics/">GoLanguageBasics</category><description>&lt;p&gt;What is the output of the following code?&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;func main() {&#10; var ans float64 = 15 &amp;#43; 25 &amp;#43; 5.2&#10; fmt.Println(ans)&#10;}&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#10;&lt;details&gt;&#10; &lt;summary&gt;🔑 Answer Analysis:&lt;/summary&gt;&#10; &lt;p&gt;Answer: 45.2&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;/details&gt;</description></item><item><title>Go Daily Question 118</title><link>https://loommii.github.io/en/go_practise/go_daily_question_118/</link><pubDate>Fri, 06 Dec 2024 23:43:00 +0800</pubDate><guid>https://loommii.github.io/en/go_practise/go_daily_question_118/</guid><category domain="https://loommii.github.io/en/categories/godailyquestion/">GoDailyQuestion</category><category domain="https://loommii.github.io/en/categories/golanguagebasics/">GoLanguageBasics</category><description>&lt;p&gt;A tweet published by Go101. Will the following code run normally, and what is the result?&#10;What is the output of the following code?&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;package main&#10;&#10;func main() {&#10; v := new(int)&#10; *v = 2&#10; println(5/&amp;#43;-*v)&#10;}&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#10;&lt;details&gt;&#10; &lt;summary&gt;🔑 Answer Analysis:&lt;/summary&gt;&#10; &lt;p&gt;Reference Answer and Analysis: B.&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;Go has the following unary operators:&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;unary_op = &amp;#34;&amp;#43;&amp;#34; | &amp;#34;-&amp;#34; | &amp;#34;!&amp;#34; | &amp;#34;^&amp;#34; | &amp;#34;*&amp;#34; | &amp;#34;&amp;amp;&amp;#34; | &amp;#34;&amp;lt;-&amp;#34; .&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;p&gt;Therefore, &lt;code&gt;5/+-*v&lt;/code&gt; will be calculated first.&#10;Also, according to the specification:&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Go Daily Question 081</title><link>https://loommii.github.io/en/go_practise/go_daily_question_081/</link><pubDate>Fri, 06 Dec 2024 20:45:06 +0800</pubDate><guid>https://loommii.github.io/en/go_practise/go_daily_question_081/</guid><category domain="https://loommii.github.io/en/categories/godailyquestion/">GoDailyQuestion</category><category domain="https://loommii.github.io/en/categories/golanguagebasics/">GoLanguageBasics</category><description>&lt;p&gt;What is the output of the following code?&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;package main&#10;&#10;import (&#10; &amp;#34;fmt&amp;#34;&#10;)&#10;&#10;func main() {&#10; var ans float64 = 15 &amp;#43; 25 &amp;#43; 5.2&#10; fmt.Println(ans)&#10;}&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;p&gt;A: Cannot compile; B: 45; C: 45.2; D: 45.0&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&#10;&lt;details&gt;&#10; &lt;summary&gt;🔑 Answer Analysis:&lt;/summary&gt;&#10; &lt;p&gt;C&#10;Numbers are untyped constants.&#10;Or rather, they are arbitrary types.&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;/details&gt;</description></item><item><title>Go Daily Question 069</title><link>https://loommii.github.io/en/go_practise/go_daily_question_069/</link><pubDate>Fri, 06 Dec 2024 20:44:52 +0800</pubDate><guid>https://loommii.github.io/en/go_practise/go_daily_question_069/</guid><category domain="https://loommii.github.io/en/categories/godailyquestion/">GoDailyQuestion</category><category domain="https://loommii.github.io/en/categories/golanguagebasics/">GoLanguageBasics</category><description>&lt;p&gt;Consider the following 3 lines of code:&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;// On a 32-bit machine&#10;1）var x int32 = 32.0&#10;2）var y int = x&#10;3）var z rune = x&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;p&gt;Will they compile? Why?&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;If asked this question in an interview, you need to consider what the interviewer wants to test you on.&#10;&#10;&lt;details&gt;&#10; &lt;summary&gt;🔑 Answer Analysis:&lt;/summary&gt;&#10; &lt;h1 class="heading-element" id="numeric-literals"&gt;&lt;span&gt;Numeric Literals&lt;/span&gt;&#10; &lt;a href="#numeric-literals" class="heading-mark"&gt;&#10; &lt;svg class="octicon octicon-link" viewBox="0 0 16 16" version="1.1" width="16" height="16" aria-hidden="true"&gt;&lt;path d="m7.775 3.275 1.25-1.25a3.5 3.5 0 1 1 4.95 4.95l-2.5 2.5a3.5 3.5 0 0 1-4.95 0 .751.751 0 0 1 .018-1.042.751.751 0 0 1 1.042-.018 1.998 1.998 0 0 0 2.83 0l2.5-2.5a2.002 2.002 0 0 0-2.83-2.83l-1.25 1.25a.751.751 0 0 1-1.042-.018.751.751 0 0 1-.018-1.042Zm-4.69 9.64a1.998 1.998 0 0 0 2.83 0l1.25-1.25a.751.751 0 0 1 1.042.018.751.751 0 0 1 .018 1.042l-1.25 1.25a3.5 3.5 0 1 1-4.95-4.95l2.5-2.5a3.5 3.5 0 0 1 4.95 0 .751.751 0 0 1-.018 1.042.751.751 0 0 1-1.042.018 1.998 1.998 0 0 0-2.83 0l-2.5 2.5a1.998 1.998 0 0 0 0 2.83Z"&gt;&lt;/path&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;&#10; &lt;/a&gt;&#10;&lt;/h1&gt;&lt;p&gt;In Go, literals are untyped. What does untyped mean? Untyped means they can be assigned to variables or constants of similar types. In the example above, &lt;code&gt;32.0&lt;/code&gt; is an untyped floating-point literal, so it can be assigned to any numeric type variable (or constant). All of the following are legal:&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Go Daily Question 051</title><link>https://loommii.github.io/en/go_practise/go_daily_question_051/</link><pubDate>Fri, 06 Dec 2024 20:44:30 +0800</pubDate><guid>https://loommii.github.io/en/go_practise/go_daily_question_051/</guid><category domain="https://loommii.github.io/en/categories/godailyquestion/">GoDailyQuestion</category><category domain="https://loommii.github.io/en/categories/golanguagebasics/">GoLanguageBasics</category><description>&lt;p&gt;What is the output of the following code?&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;func main() { &#10; i := -5&#10; j := &amp;#43;5&#10; fmt.Printf(&amp;#34;%&amp;#43;d %&amp;#43;d&amp;#34;, i, j)&#10;}&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;p&gt;A. -5 +5&#10;B. +5 +5&#10;C. 0 0&#10;&#10;&lt;details&gt;&#10; &lt;summary&gt;🔑 Answer Analysis:&lt;/summary&gt;&#10; &lt;p&gt;A&#10;&lt;code&gt;%d&lt;/code&gt; indicates outputting a decimal number, and &lt;code&gt;+&lt;/code&gt; indicates outputting the sign of the number. It does not mean negation here.&#10;&lt;code&gt;%d&lt;/code&gt; output for positive numbers will not include the &lt;code&gt;+&lt;/code&gt; sign by default.&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;/details&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</description></item></channel></rss>